<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zagwe dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from c. 940 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Zagwe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zagwe dynasty was a ruling dynasty of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> that rose to power after the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, from around 940 CE according to the chronology. It is best known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">rock-hewn churches</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>, built under its king of that name. The dynasty was overthrown in 1270 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zagwe dynasty" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taddesse Tamrat · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZagweMap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
